<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of Ireland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of Ireland is the Anglican church in Ireland. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> it became the established state church, though most of the population remained Catholic, and it held the ancient cathedrals and parish revenues, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> paid by Catholics. It was united with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> in 1801 and disestablished in 1871, and today it is organised on an all-Ireland basis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland.jpg" alt="Church of Ireland" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JohnArmagh (shifted &amp; cropped by Rabanus Flavus) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armagh_Cathedral_(Church_of_Ireland).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Church of Ireland sees itself as the part of the Irish Church that was influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, with its origins in the early Celtic Church of Saint Patrick. It therefore regards itself as both catholic, as the inheritor of a continuous tradition, and protestant, since it rejects the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Christianity in Ireland is generally dated to the mid to late 5th century, when the Romano-British cleric Saint Patrick began his mission, although the exact dates are disputed.</p><p>Before the 12th-century reforms the Irish church was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> and in communion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, though major monasteries held considerable authority. After the Synod of Ráth Breasail in 1111 and the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> in 1152 it was organised by dioceses and parishes, and the Primacy of Ireland went to the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the beginning of the 17th century most native Irish were Catholic, while Protestant settlers in Ulster established an independent Presbyterian church. The most important figure in the Church's development was the Dublin-born theologian and historian James Ussher, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> from 1625 to 1656. In 1615 the Church drew up its own confession of faith, similar to the English one but more detailed and often explicitly Calvinist. The Thirty-Nine Articles were formally adopted in 1634 and soon superseded the Irish Articles.</p><p>In 1704 the Test Act was extended to Ireland, which restricted public office to members of the Church of Ireland until the Catholic Relief Act of 1829. It is estimated that fewer than 15 to 20 per cent of the population were nominally members. After the Act of Union 1800 the Church was united with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and one archbishop and three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from Ireland, chosen by rotation, sat in the House of Lords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Church of Ireland did not divide when Ireland was partitioned in the 1920s and is still governed on an all-Ireland basis. Its head is, ex officio, the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>. Its polity is episcopal, with parishes grouped into dioceses; there are now eleven dioceses or united dioceses in two provinces. On its disestablishment it adopted synodical government, the second province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Anglican Church</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> to do so.</p><p>It has two cathedrals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, Christ Church Cathedral and Saint Patrick's Cathedral, the latter designated the National Cathedral for Ireland in 1870. It began ordaining women to the priesthood in 1990, and in 2013 appointed its first female <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Pat Storey. The first translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> into Irish was published in 1606.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church of Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Church of Ireland</b> was the established church, though the great majority of the population remained Catholic, and Ulster Presbyterians formed a large dissenting community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Church of Ireland</b> was united with the Church of England.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
